{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787507927406,"sessionID":"ses_fd03a4315ffec31s0GggcSnBoE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"bash:12","state":{"status":"error","input":{"command":"node --version && cat package.json","workdir":"/private/var/folders/cs/y_h_9x9j2hj316xhs3mvszd80000gn/T/coding-assistant-workflow-o0L2GK/checkout"},"error":"The user has specified a rule which prevents you from using this specific tool call. Here are some of the relevant rules [{\"permission\":\"*\",\"action\":\"allow\",\"pattern\":\"*\"},{\"permission\":\"*\",\"action\":\"deny\",\"pattern\":\"*\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"*\",\"action\":\"deny\"},{\"permission\":\"bash\",\"pattern\":\"node --test benchmark/fixtures/T5-review-queue/review-queue.test.mjs benchmark/fixtures/T5-review-queue/student-tests.mjs\",\"action\":\"allow\"}]","time":{"start":1787507927368,"end":1787507927405}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The prompt matches the user's message. Now let me implement the five files, in dependency order: store → API → page → student tests → README.\n\n### 1. `review-store.mjs`\n\n```js\nconst REVIEW_FIELDS = [\"runId\", \"decision\", \"reviewer\", \"note\"];\n\nfunction defaultNow() {\n  return new Date().toISOString();\n}\n\nfunction isPlainRecord(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"object\" && value !== null && !Array.isArray(value);\n}\n\nfunction copyReview(review) {\n  return {\n    runId: review.runId,\n    decision: review.decision,\n    reviewer: review.reviewer,\n    note: review.note,\n    createdAt: review.createdAt,\n    updatedAt: review.updatedAt,\n  };\n}\n\nexport function createReviewStore({ now } = {}) {\n  const clock = now === undefined ? defaultNow : now;\n  if (typeof clock !== \"function\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"now must be a function\");\n  }\n\n  const reviews = new Map();\n\n  function save(input) {\n    if (!isPlainRecord(input)) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"review must be an object\");\n    }\n    for (const field of REVIEW_FIELDS) {\n      if (typeof input[field] !== \"string\") {\n        throw new TypeError(`review.${field} must be a string`);\n      }\n    }\n\n    const timestamp = clock();\n    if (typeof timestamp !== \"string\" || timestamp.length === 0) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"now must return a non-empty string\");\n    }\n\n    const existing = reviews.get(input.runId);\n    const created = existing === undefined;\n    const stored = {\n      runId: input.runId,\n      decision: input.decision,\n      reviewer: input.reviewer,\n      note: input.note,\n      createdAt: created ? timestamp : existing.createdAt,\n      updatedAt: timestamp,\n    };\n    reviews.set(input.runId, stored);\n    return { created, review: copyReview(stored) };\n  }\n\n  function get(runId) {\n    const stored = reviews.get(runId);\n    return stored === undefined ? null : copyReview(stored);\n  }\n\n  function list({ decision } = {}) {\n    const all = [...reviews.values()];\n    const filtered = decision === undefined ? all : all.filter((review) => review.decision === decision);\n    const sorted = filtered.sort((a, b) => {\n      if (a.updatedAt < b.updatedAt) return 1;\n      if (a.updatedAt > b.updatedAt) return -1;\n      if (a.runId < b.runId) return -1;\n      if (a.runId > b.runId) return 1;\n      return 0;\n    });\n    return sorted.map(copyReview);\n  }\n\n  return { save, get, list };\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `save` validates input fully before calling `now()` — the plan says \"validate the input fully *before* calling `now()`; call it exactly once\". Yes.\n- If `now()` returns a bad value, throw TypeError without mutating the map. In my code, the timestamp check happens before `reviews.set`, so no mutation. Good.\n- Sort: descending updatedAt, ascending runId, using `<`/`>`.\n- Copies in and out: input is copied into a fresh `stored` object (only the six fields); returned reviews are copies.\n\nWait — one subtlety on the ordering of operations in `save`: If the clock returns a bad timestamp on an update, we must not mutate the map. In my code, I check the timestamp before `reviews.set`, so the existing entry stays intact. Good.\n\nAlso, for a tie in `updatedAt` — sort must be stable? Not necessarily, since I explicitly tie-break on runId.\n\n### 2. `review-api.mjs`\n\n```js\nconst JSON_CONTENT_TYPE = \"application/json; charset=utf-8\";\nconst VALID_DECISIONS = [\"approve\", \"reject\", \"needs-work\"];\nconst BODY_FIELDS = [\"runId\", \"decision\", \"reviewer\", \"note\"];\nconst RUN_ID_PATTERN = /^run-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{2,63}$/;\nconst REVIEWER_MIN = 2;\nconst REVIEWER_MAX = 40;\nconst NOTE_MAX = 240;\n\nfunction respond(status, body, extraHeaders = {}) {\n  return {\n    status,\n    headers: { \"content-type\": JSON_CONTENT_TYPE, ...extraHeaders },\n    body,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction validationError(fields) {\n  return respond(400, { error: \"validation_failed\", fields });\n}\n\nfunction invalidRequest() {\n  return respond(400, { error: \"invalid_request\" });\n}\n\nfunction isPlainRecord(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"object\" && value !== null && !Array.isArray(value);\n}\n\nexport function createReviewApi(store) {\n  if (!isPlainRecord(store) && typeof store !== \"function\") { ... }\n```\n\nHmm, simpler: check `store == null` or missing methods:\n\n```js\nif (\n  store === null ||\n  typeof store !== \"object\" && typeof store !== \"function\" ||\n  ...\n```\n\nActually, just check the three methods:\n\n```js\nif (\n  store == null ||\n  typeof store.save !== \"function\" ||\n  typeof store.get !== \"function\" ||\n  typeof store.list !== \"function\"\n) {\n  throw new TypeError(\"store must expose save, get, and list functions\");\n}\n```\n\nThat handles null/undefined (== null covers both) and any store without callable members.\n\nNow the handler:\n\n```js\nfunction handleReviewRequest(request) {\n  if (!isPlainRecord(request)) {\n    return invalidRequest();\n  }\n  const { method, url } = request;\n  if (typeof method !== \"string\" || typeof url !== \"string\" || url.length === 0) {\n    return invalidRequest();\n  }\n\n  let parsed;\n  let pathname;\n  let searchParams;\n  try {\n    parsed = new URL(url, \"http://localhost\");\n    pathname = parsed.pathname;\n    searchParams = parsed.searchParams;\n  } catch {\n    return invalidRequest();\n  }\n```\n\nWait — `new URL(url, \"http://localhost\")` — what urls would throw? A relative URL parses fine with a base. Something like \"http://[::1\" would throw. Also control characters... Let's keep try/catch.\n\nRoute matching: strip one trailing slash. So:\n\n```js\nlet routePath = pathname;\nif (routePath.length > 1 && routePath.endsWith(\"/\")) {\n  routePath = routePath.slice(0, -1);\n}\n```\n\nHmm — should I strip one trailing slash or all? Plan says \"Strip one trailing slash, so `/api/reviews/` is the collection.\" Strip one. `/api/reviews//` → after stripping one: `/api/reviews/` → not equal to `/api/reviews`, then it would try detail matching: segments after `/api/reviews/` would be empty → unknown route 404. Fine.\n\nCollection: `routePath === \"/api/reviews\"`.\nDetail: `routePath.startsWith(\"/api/reviews/\")` and the remainder is exactly one non-empty segment (no `/` inside).\n\n```js\nconst upperMethod = method.toUpperCase();\n\nif (routePath === COLLECTION_PATH) {\n  if (upperMethod === \"POST\") return handlePost(request.body);\n  if (upperMethod === \"GET\") return handleList(searchParams);\n  return respond(405, { error: \"method_not_allowed\" }, { allow: \"GET, POST\" });\n}\n\nif (routePath.startsWith(DETAIL_PREFIX)) {\n  const rawId = routePath.slice(DETAIL_PREFIX.length);\n  if (rawId.length > 0 && !rawId.includes(\"/\")) {\n    let runId;\n    try {\n      runId = decodeURIComponent(rawId);\n    } catch {\n      return invalidRequest();\n    }\n    if (upperMethod !== \"GET\") {\n      return respond(405, { error: \"method_not_allowed\" }, { allow: \"GET\" });\n    }\n    const review = store.get(runId);\n    if (review === null) {\n      return respond(404, { error: \"review_not_found\" });\n    }\n    return respond(200, { review });\n  }\n}\n\nreturn respond(404, { error: \"not_found\" });\n```\n\nHmm, wait — should the detail 405 come before or after decoding? If the method is DELETE on `/api/reviews/run-1`, it's a recognized route with an unsupported method → 405. Decoding first is fine since \"run-1\" decodes fine. For a malformed percent-encoding like `/api/reviews/%E0%A4%A` with GET, decodeURIComponent throws → invalid_request 400. That seems reasonable per the plan.\n\nAlso, should 405 for detail apply when the runId is malformed? Edge case, not specified. My order: decode first (may return invalid_request), then method check → 405. Either order is defensible.\n\nHmm, one question: should the detail route check method before or after store.get? Definitely method first — a DELETE on an existing review should be 405, not something else. And a DELETE on a nonexistent review should also be 405 (route recognized). Yes, method check first, then get.\n\nFor the collection POST:\n\n```js\nfunction handlePost(body) {\n  const fields = {};\n\n  if (!isPlainRecord(body)) {\n    return validationError({ body: \"Body must be an object with runId, decision, reviewer, and note\" });\n  }\n\n  const unknown = Object.keys(body).filter((key) => !BODY_FIELDS.includes(key)).sort();\n  if (unknown.length > 0) {\n    fields.body = `Unknown fields: ${unknown.join(\", \")}`;\n  }\n\n  const values = {};\n  for (const field of BODY_FIELDS) {\n    const value = body[field];\n    if (typeof value !== \"string\") {\n      fields[field] = `${field} must be a string`;\n    } else {\n      values[field] = value.trim();\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (values.runId !== undefined && !RUN_ID_PATTERN.test(values.runId)) {\n    fields.runId = \"runId must match run-<id> (lowercase letters, digits, and hyphens)\";\n  }\n  if (values.decision !== undefined && !VALID_DECISIONS.includes(values.decision)) {\n    fields.decision = \"decision must be approve, reject, or needs-work\";\n  }\n  if (values.reviewer !== undefined && (values.reviewer.length < REVIEWER_MIN || values.reviewer.length > REVIEWER_MAX)) {\n    fields.reviewer = \"reviewer must contain 2 through 40 characters\";\n  }\n  if (values.note !== undefined) {\n    if (values.note.length > NOTE_MAX) {\n      fields.note = \"note must be at most 240 characters\";\n    } else if (values.note.length === 0 && (values.decision === \"reject\" || values.decision === \"needs-work\")) {\n      fields.note = \"note is required for reject and needs-work\";\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (Object.keys(fields).length > 0) {\n    return validationError(fields);\n  }\n\n  const { created, review } = store.save(values);\n  return respond(created ? 201 : 200, { review });\n}\n```\n\nWait — check the visible test: body `{ runId: \"bad\", decision: \"reject\", reviewer: \"A\", note: \"\" }` → expected field keys sorted: `[\"note\", \"reviewer\", \"runId\"]`. \n\n- runId \"bad\": matches the pattern? \"bad\" doesn't start with \"run-\" → error. ✓\n- decision \"reject\": valid → no error.\n- reviewer \"A\": length 1 < 2 → error. ✓\n- note \"\": decision is reject, note empty → error. ✓\n\nSo fields = { runId, reviewer, note } → sorted [\"note\", \"reviewer\", \"runId\"]. ✓\n\nNote-required rule: applied when the *trimmed decision* is reject/needs-work. What if the decision is invalid (e.g., \"maybe\") and the note is empty? Then decision error is reported; note-required doesn't apply since decision isn't reject/needs-work. That's fine — we don't double-report.\n\nHmm, but one subtlety: should the note-required check use `values.decision` (trimmed) — yes, \"Trim all four string values before validation and storage.\"\n\nAlso important: never call `store.save` with invalid data. In my flow, if fields has any errors → return before save. But also — what if unknown fields exist but everything else is valid? fields.body is set → validation error → no save. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: `store.save(values)` — values only contains the four trimmed fields; store persists only those plus timestamps. The response `{ review }` — review has the six keys. The visible test deepEquals against exactly six keys. My store's copyReview returns exactly six keys. ✓\n\nFor GET collection:\n\n```js\nfunction handleList(searchParams) {\n  const all = store.list();\n  const summary = { total: all.length, approve: 0, reject: 0, \"needs-work\": 0 };\n  for (const review of all) {\n    if (review.decision === \"approve\") summary.approve += 1;\n    else if (review.decision === \"reject\") summary.reject += 1;\n    else if (review.decision === \"needs-work\") summary[\"needs-work\"] += 1;\n  }\n\n  let reviews = all;\n  if (searchParams.has(\"decision\")) {\n    const decision = searchParams.get(\"decision\");\n    if (!VALID_DECISIONS.includes(decision)) {\n      return validationError({ decision: \"decision must be approve, reject, or needs-work\" });\n    }\n    reviews = all.filter((review) => review.decision === decision);\n  }\n\n  return respond(200, { reviews, summary });\n}\n```\n\nHmm — summary computed from store.list() unfiltered; but note: what if stored decisions include something weird (only possible if store was populated out-of-band)? The summary counts only the three known decisions; total counts all. That matches \"always counts all stored reviews and has exactly total, approve, reject, and needs-work.\"\n\nNote: should I call `store.list()` once and filter in memory, or call `store.list({ decision })` for the filtered set? Either works. The plan says \"obtains the unfiltered list once for summary calculation... filter only reviews\". Filtering in memory from the same list is fine and keeps ordering consistent.\n\nHmm wait, one thing about the summary when the query decision is invalid — return validation error, no summary. Fine.\n\nEmpty `?decision=` — searchParams.has(\"decision\") is true, value \"\" → invalid → validation error. Per plan. ✓\n\nResponse shape: exactly `{ status, headers, body }`. My `respond` helper returns exactly those three keys, fresh headers object each time. ✓\n\nNow — `handleReviewRequest` must be returned directly: `return handleReviewRequest;` The visible test does `const handle = createReviewApi(store); handle({...})`. ✓\n\nAlso: the handler should never throw. The store could theoretically throw if save fails — but by the time we call save, all inputs are validated strings, so the store won't throw TypeError. The clock could throw though (default clock won't). If a custom clock throws... that would propagate. The contract says \"An unusable request object or URL returns status 400 ... rather than throwing\" — only request/URL issues. I won't wrap store calls in try/catch; a throwing clock is a store-side concern. Actually, hmm — should I be defensive? The plan doesn't require it. Keep it simple.\n\nOne more route consideration: `GET /api/reviews` with the URL exactly `\"/api/reviews\"` — pathname is \"/api/reviews\". ✓. What about absolute URL \"http://example.com/api/reviews\"? new URL with base handles it; pathname extracted. ✓.\n\nWhat about `request.body` missing (undefined) for POST? → not a plain record → validation error with fields.body. Per corrected plan. ✓\n\nEdge: what about a POST to a detail route `/api/reviews/run-1`? Detail route with method POST → 405 allow: GET. ✓\n\nWhat about a method like \"get\" lowercase? We uppercase → GET. Reasonable normalization (\"Normalize methods to uppercase if desired\").\n\n### 3. `review-page.mjs`\n\nRequirements from visible test:\n- `/<main>/` — attribute-free main tag.\n- `/<label for=\"run-id\">Run ID<\\/label>/` — exact.\n- `/aria-live=\"polite\"/`\n- `/<caption>Saved run reviews<\\/caption>/`\n- `/No reviews yet\\./`\n\nFull contract:\n- Complete HTML document.\n- One main heading (h1), short explanation, main landmark.\n- Show all four summary values.\n- POST form for four fields; every control has a matching label; describe when the note is required.\n- message in polite live status region.\n- Table with caption and column headers: run ID, decision, reviewer, note, updated timestamp. Empty state \"No reviews yet.\"\n- HTML-escape every dynamic value.\n\n```js\nconst ESCAPES = {\n  \"&\": \"&amp;\",\n  \"<\": \"&lt;\",\n  \">\": \"&gt;\",\n  '\"': \"&quot;\",\n  \"'\": \"&#39;\",\n};\n\nfunction escapeHtml(value) {\n  return String(value).replace(/[&<>\"']/g, (char) => ESCAPES[char]);\n}\n\nfunction renderSummary(summary) {\n  const safe = summary && typeof summary === \"object\" ? summary : {};\n  const entries = [\n    [\"Total\", safe.total],\n    [\"Approved\", safe.approve],\n    [\"Rejected\", safe.reject],\n    [\"Needs work\", safe[\"needs-work\"]],\n  ];\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm — summary values: escape `String(value)`; if undefined → \"undefined\"? Better to default to 0. Plan: \"Default `reviews = []` and `summary = {}` so a partial call cannot throw.\" For missing summary values, display 0. Let me use `value === undefined ? 0 : value`... Actually, escaping handles any type; displaying \"0\" for missing values is friendlier. Let me write a small helper:\n\n```js\nfunction summaryValue(summary, key) {\n  const value = summary[key];\n  return value === undefined || value === null ? 0 : value;\n}\n```\n\nSignature: `renderReviewPage({ reviews, summary, message = \"\" })`. If called with no args at all, destructuring would throw. Plan says default reviews=[] and summary={}. Should I also default the whole parameter `= {}`? That makes `renderReviewPage()` work. Not required but harmless and defensive. I'll add `= {}`.\n\nForm: method=\"post\" action=\"/api/reviews\". Controls with ids: run-id, decision, reviewer, note; names: runId, decision, reviewer, note. Labels exactly `<label for=\"run-id\">Run ID</label>` etc. For decision — a select with the three options, or an input? A select is nicer. The label matches via for/id.\n\nNote guidance: \"describe when the note is required\" — e.g., `<p id=\"note-help\">A note is required when rejecting a run or marking it as needs-work; it is optional for approvals.</p>` and link with aria-describedby=\"note-help\" on the note control.\n\nMessage region: `<p class=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\" role=\"status\">{escapeHtml(message)}</p>` — rendered always, even when empty.\n\nTable:\n\n```html\n<table>\n  <caption>Saved run reviews</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th scope=\"col\">Run ID</th>\n      <th scope=\"col\">Decision</th>\n      <th scope=\"col\">Reviewer</th>\n      <th scope=\"col\">Note</th>\n      <th scope=\"col\">Updated</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    ...rows or <tr><td colspan=\"5\">No reviews yet.</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n```\n\nReviews might be non-array if caller passes garbage — default to [] and guard with Array.isArray.\n\nFor each review, escape all five fields. Use `review && typeof review === \"object\" ? review : {}` guard? Keep it simple: iterate if Array.isArray; for each review, access fields with fallbacks. I'll do a small normalization: `const safe = typeof review === \"object\" && review !== null ? review : {};` then escape each of the five with `escapeHtml(safe.runId ?? \"\")`... hmm, `??` is fine in modern Node. Let me check node version in the repo to be safe.\n\nLet me now write the page with template literal assembly.\n\nDoctype: `<!DOCTYPE html>`, `<html lang=\"en\">`, head with meta charset and title, body > main.\n\nThe regex `/<main>/` requires literally `<main>` — so no attributes on main. ✓\n\n### 4. `student-tests.mjs`\n\nFour tests per the checklist:\n\n(a) store: tie-break ordering, filtering, copy isolation both directions, bad-clock TypeError.\n\n```js\ntest(\"store sorts by newest update, breaks ties by runId, filters, and isolates state\", () => {\n  const store = createReviewStore({ now: () => \"2026-08-23T09:00:00.000Z\" });\n  // hmm, for tie-break I need same timestamps\n```\n\nLet me design: clock returning the same timestamp for several saves → ties broken by runId ascending. Then one save with a later timestamp → newest first.\n\nUse a controllable clock: `let current = \"t1\"; const store = createReviewStore({ now: () => current });`\n\n```js\nconst store = createReviewStore({ now: () => current });\ncurrent = \"2026-08-23T10:00:00.000Z\";\nstore.save({ runId: \"run-bravo\", decision: \"approve\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"\" });\nstore.save({ runId: \"run-alpha\", decision: \"reject\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"No\" });\nstore.save({ runId: \"run-charlie\", decision: \"approve\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"\" });\n// tie at same timestamp → alpha, bravo, charlie ascending runId\nlet ids = store.list().map((r) => r.runId);\nassert.deepEqual(ids, [\"run-alpha\", \"run-bravo\", \"run-charlie\"]);\n\ncurrent = \"2026-08-23T11:00:00.000Z\";\nstore.save({ runId: \"run-bravo\", decision: \"needs-work\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"Fix\" });\nids = store.list().map((r) => r.runId);\nassert.deepEqual(ids, [\"run-bravo\", \"run-alpha\", \"run-charlie\"]);\n\n// filter\nconst approves = store.list({ decision: \"approve\" });\nassert.deepEqual(approves.map((r) => r.runId), [\"run-alpha\", \"run-charlie\"]);\n\n// copy isolation, outbound\nconst listed = store.list();\nlisted[0].note = \"hacked\";\nassert.notEqual(store.get(\"run-bravo\").note, \"hacked\");\n\n// inbound\nconst input = { runId: \"run-delta\", decision: \"approve\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"ok\" };\nstore.save(input);\ninput.note = \"changed after save\";\nassert.equal(store.get(\"run-delta\").note, \"ok\");\n```\n\nAnd bad clock:\n\n```js\ntest(\"store rejects a non-function clock and bad clock results without mutating state\", () => {\n  assert.throws(() => createReviewStore({ now: \"nope\" }), TypeError);\n\n  let calls = 0;\n  const store = createReviewStore({ now: () => { calls += 1; return calls === 1 ? \"2026-08-23T10:00:00.000Z\" : \"\"; } });\n  store.save({ runId: \"run-alpha\", decision: \"approve\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"\" });\n  assert.throws(() => store.save({ runId: \"run-alpha\", decision: \"reject\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"x\" }), TypeError);\n  // state unchanged\n  assert.equal(store.get(\"run-alpha\").decision, \"approve\");\n  assert.equal(store.get(\"run-alpha\").updatedAt, \"2026-08-23T10:00:00.000Z\");\n});\n```\n\nAlso maybe assert non-object input and non-string fields TypeErrors. Could fold into the same test.\n\n(b) API: update returns 200 with preserved createdAt; sorted unknown-field message; invalid input not saved.\n\n```js\ntest(\"api updates keep createdAt, report sorted unknown fields, and never save invalid data\", () => {\n  let current = \"2026-08-23T10:00:00.000Z\";\n  const store = createReviewStore({ now: () => current });\n  const handle = createReviewApi(store);\n\n  const created = handle({ method: \"POST\", url: \"/api/reviews\", body: { runId: \"run-alpha\", decision: \"approve\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"\" } });\n  assert.equal(created.status, 201);\n\n  current = \"2026-08-23T11:00:00.000Z\";\n  const updated = handle({ method: \"POST\", url: \"/api/reviews\", body: { runId: \"run-alpha\", decision: \"reject\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"Missing tests\" } });\n  assert.equal(updated.status, 200);\n  assert.equal(updated.body.review.createdAt, \"2026-08-23T10:00:00.000Z\");\n  assert.equal(updated.body.review.updatedAt, \"2026-08-23T11:00:00.000Z\");\n  assert.equal(updated.body.review.decision, \"reject\");\n\n  const unknown = handle({ method: \"POST\", url: \"/api/reviews\", body: { runId: \"run-beta\", decision: \"approve\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"\", zeta: 1, alpha: 2 } });\n  assert.equal(unknown.status, 400);\n  assert.equal(unknown.body.error, \"validation_failed\");\n  assert.match(unknown.body.fields.body, /alpha/);\n  // check sorted: index of alpha before zeta\n  assert.ok(unknown.body.fields.body.indexOf(\"alpha\") < unknown.body.fields.body.indexOf(\"zeta\"));\n  assert.equal(store.get(\"run-beta\"), null);\n\n  // invalid data not saved\n  const invalid = handle({ method: \"POST\", url: \"/api/reviews\", body: { runId: \"bad\", decision: \"approve\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"\" } });\n  assert.equal(invalid.status, 400);\n  assert.deepEqual(store.list().map((r) => r.runId), [\"run-alpha\"]);\n});\n```\n\n(c) API error paths: 405 allow values, detail 404, invalid_request for non-object request, no header leak after 405, filtered reviews vs global summary.\n\n```js\ntest(\"api enforces methods, detail lookups, and keeps summaries global under filters\", () => {\n  const store = createReviewStore({ now: () => \"2026-08-23T12:00:00.000Z\" });\n  const handle = createReviewApi(store);\n  handle({ method: \"POST\", url: \"/api/reviews\", body: { runId: \"run-alpha\", decision: \"approve\", reviewer: \"Ada\", note: \"\" } });\n  handle({ method: \"POST\", url: \"/api/reviews\", body: { runId: \"run-beta\", decision: \"reject\", reviewer: \"Bob\", note: \"Flaky\" } });\n\n  const filtered = handle({ method: \"GET\", url: \"/api/reviews?decision=reject\" });\n  assert.equal(filtered.status, 200);\n  assert.deepEqual(filtered.body.reviews.map((r) => r.runId), [\"run-beta\"]);\n  assert.deepEqual(filtered.body.summary, { total: 2, approve: 1, reject: 1, \"needs-work\": 0 });\n\n  const badFilter = handle({ method: \"GET\", url: \"/api/reviews?decision=mixed\" });\n  assert.equal(badFilter.status, 400);\n  assert.equal(badFilter.body.error, \"validation_failed\");\n\n  const missing = handle({ method: \"GET\", url: \"/api/reviews/run-missing\" });\n  assert.equal(missing.status, 404);\n  assert.deepEqual(missing.body, { error: \"review_not_found\" });\n\n  const detail = handle({ method: \"GET\", url: \"/api/reviews/run-alpha\" });\n  assert.equal(detail.status, 200);\n  assert.equal(detail.body.review.runId, \"run-alpha\");\n\n  const notAllowed = handle({ method: \"DELETE\", url: \"/api/reviews\" });\n  assert.equal(notAllowed.status, 405);\n  assert.equal(notAllowed.body.error, \"method_not_allowed\");\n  assert.equal(notAllowed.headers.allow, \"GET, POST\");\n\n  const detailNotAllowed = handle({ method: \"POST\", url: \"/api/reviews/run-alpha\" });\n  assert.equal(detailNotAllowed.status, 405);\n  assert.equal(detailNotAllowed.headers.allow, \"GET\");\n\n  // 405 must not leak allow into the next response\n  const after = handle({ method: \"GET\", url: \"/api/reviews/run-alpha\" });\n  assert.equal(\"allow\" in after.headers, false);\n  assert.deepEqual(Object.keys(after), [\"status\", \"headers\", \"body\"]);\n\n  const unknown = handle({ method: \"GET\", url: \"/api/unknown\" });\n  assert.equal(unknown.status, 404);\n  assert.deepEqual(unknown.body, { error: \"not_found\" });\n\n  assert.deepEqual(handle(null).body, { error: \"invalid_request\" });\n  assert.deepEqual(handle({ method: \"GET\", url: 42 }).body, { error: \"invalid_request\" });\n  assert.equal(handle(null).status, 400);\n});\n```\n\nHmm wait, `Object.keys(after)` — order of keys: respond returns `{ status, headers, body }` — insertion order is status, headers, body. deepEqual on arrays checks order too. Fine since I construct in that order.\n\n(d) Page escaping:\n\n```js\ntest(\"page escapes message, summary, and review fields against injection\", () => {\n  const html = renderReviewPage({\n    reviews: [\n      {\n        runId: 'run-x\"><script>alert(1)</script>',\n        decision: \"reject\",\n        reviewer: '<img src=x onerror=\"alert(1)\">',\n        note: '\" onload=\"alert(1)',\n        createdAt: \"2026-08-23T10:00:00.000Z\",\n        updatedAt: \"2026-08-23T11:00:00.000Z\",\n      },\n    ],\n    summary: { total: \"<b>1</b>\", approve: 1, reject: 1, \"needs-work\": 0 },\n    message: '<script>alert(\"msg\")</script>',\n  });\n\n  assert.equal(html.includes(\"<script>\"), false);\n  assert.equal(html.includes(\"<img\"), false);\n  assert.equal(html.includes('onload=\"alert'), false);\n  assert.ok(html.includes(\"&lt;script&gt;\"));\n  ...\n});\n```\n\nWait careful: `onload=\"alert(1)\"` escaped → the `\"` becomes `&quot;`, so the raw string `onload=\"alert` (with literal quote) won't appear. But `onload=` alone... In my escape, `'\" onload=\"alert(1)'` becomes `&quot; onload=&quot;alert(1)`. So `html.includes('onload=\"alert')` → false. Good. But `html.includes(\"onload=\")` would be true (harmless text). Only assert on the dangerous patterns with quotes.\n\nAlso `<b>1</b>` in summary → check `html.includes(\"<b>1</b>\") === false` and `html.includes(\"&lt;b&gt;1&lt;/b&gt;\") === true`.\n\nAlso check the raw malicious runId isn't present unescaped: `html.includes('run-x\"><script>')` false.\n\nAnd structural checks: `<label for=\"note\">Note</label>`, aria-describedby present, summary values shown. But careful not to duplicate committed tests too closely (\"Do not copy or modify the committed review-queue.test.mjs suite\" — copying cases is prohibited; my page test focuses on escaping, different from the committed structural test).\n\nAlso maybe assert that escaped message appears: `&lt;script&gt;alert(&quot;msg&quot;)&lt;/script&gt;`.\n\nHmm — one more consideration on test (a): the committed suite already covers created/updatedAt basics. My tests focus on different aspects (tie-break, filtering, isolation, bad clock). Good.\n\nLet me also double check `assert.throws(() => createReviewStore({ now: \"nope\" }), TypeError)` — yes, constructor throws TypeError.\n\nNow, one more edge for the store: `list({ decision: undefined })`? If someone passes `{ decision: undefined }`, my filter checks `decision === undefined` → no filter. Good.\n\nWhat about `store.get()` of a missing run → null. ✓\n\n### 5. README.md\n\nDocument routes, behavior, errors, non-durability.\n\nNow, let me also check the node version available to ensure `Array.prototype.sort` stability etc. (V8 sort is stable since Node 11). Fine.\n\nAlso check: does the repo have lint/typecheck that includes these fixture files? Let me check package.json to see whether `npm run lint`/`typecheck` would touch fixtures. The task says run only the node --test command. But AGENTS.md mentions standard commands. Running lint/typecheck might fail for unrelated reasons; the task's acceptance is the node --test command. I'll run the specified command, plus maybe quickly verify node parses the files. Let me check package.json first.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_02fc6693e001R0sns2QeFyADkS","sessionID":"ses_fd03a4315ffec31s0GggcSnBoE","messageID":"msg_02fc5ea38001DEsXrhI5Gsr2lr"}}
